 "observations": [
  "The page number «180» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 66», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. The titles (L01-L02), «Gedval elbuht (CO)» and «Tabula motus Solis et Lunae diurni et horarii atque momentanei (N)», in bold, the sigla in roman; a speck before «elbuht». The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of the second title in two lines, «C 79r, O 136r,» and «N 41r.», the letters raised; here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: as on pp. 178-179 (JP2 0210-0211), the heads without the notes of C's readings except note 1 at «rotae» in the sixth head. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L03-L07); the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns.",
  "L08-L37. The arguments, the anomalies 5s 1° to 6s 0° and their complements 6s 29° to 6s 0°, with N's reading of note a at 6s 0°. At 180° (L37) the hourly motions reach their greatest values, 2′ 34″ and 35′ 40″, as Suter says (p. 78, JP2 0110, L19-L21); the radii are 16′ 54″ and 17′ 17″, the last two columns 12′ 48″ and 57′ 4″. Checked: the law 2′ 28″ − 5.8″ cos α of pp. 178-179 gives the second column within 0.7″, the sixth (five times) within 1.3″ and the fourth (6.6 times) within 1.5″; the seventh column is 1 3/5 times the third within 1.4″ and the fifth 0.4846 times the third within 0.8″ in every row; the third column agrees with 32′ 56″ 27‴ − 2′ 43.9″ cos α within 1.5″.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: the 12′ 48″ of O and N at 179° (note b, against C's 47) is the value of the column from 168° to 180°, where five times the law gives 12′ 49.0″; C's 56′ 55″ at 164° (note c, against O's 50) is 1 3/5 times the third column, 56′ 54.4″, between the 56′ 53″ and 56′ 56″ of the neighbouring rows. Note a: N's 6, against the 5 of C and O, makes the argument of L37 6s 0°, 180°, the complement of 6s 0°. Note 1: C omits «rotae» in the head of the sixth pair. Italics: «om.»."
 ],
